RENAME(2)                      P32W Unix                       RENAME(2)

NAME
       rename - change the name of a file

SYNOPSIS
       int rename(const char *old, const char *new);

       #include <stdio.h>

DESCRIPTION
       rename gives the file old the name new and removes the name
       old. If new already names a file, that entry is replaced.
       If old and new name the same file (by any path, such as a
       and ./a), nothing happens. The caller needs write permission
       on both directories; both names must be on the same
       filesystem. Only files can be renamed, not directories.

       Everything that could fail is checked before anything is
       removed, so a failed rename leaves both names as they were.
       It is then done as unlink(new); link(old, new); unlink(old)
       inside the kernel.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOENT  old does not exist, or a directory in either path
               does not exist.
       EISDIR  old is a directory.
       EISDIR  new is a directory (it is not removed).
       EXDEV   old and new are on different filesystems.
       EACCES  A directory in a path cannot be searched, or a
               directory to be changed is not writable.
       ENOTDIR A component of a path prefix is not a directory.
       ENOSPC  The directory for new cannot grow.
       EFAULT  A path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               A path is 256 characters or more, or a component
               is longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       link(2), unlink(2), mv(1)

BUGS
       It is not atomic: a crash between the steps can leave the
       file with both names, or (rarely) with only the new one.
       Directories cannot be renamed. V7 had no rename call at all.
